WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.240
Buffer safety review should be systematic and repeatable.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:11.620
Review where input enters, where lengths are calculated, where memory is allocated, where data is copied, where formatting happens, and where strings are terminated.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.960
These are the points where assumptions become memory behavior.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.260
Review every buffer contract.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:18.840
Who owns the buffer.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:20.180
How large is it.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.800
How much meaningful data does it currently contain.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.260
Is it a C string or a byte region.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.080
What happens on failure.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.020
Does the function preserve a valid output state.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.600
The answers should be visible in the interface and surrounding code.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.340
Check error paths, cleanup paths, partial writes, truncation behavior, and logging.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:49.240
A function that works on the success path can still leave a partially updated buffer, stale length field, missing terminator, or confusing status value when something fails halfway through.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:58.340
Review third-party library boundaries and assumptions about field sizes, protocol sizes, terminator behavior, allocation ownership, and returned lengths.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.200
A library may be safe under its own contract while the calling code misuses that contract or ignores an important result.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:11.420
Production diagnostics should not expose raw memory, secrets, internal paths, or sensitive data.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:22.680
Diagnostic output should identify safe context such as operation name, failure category, size category, and correlation ID without dumping arbitrary memory or private content.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.660
Defects found in production should feed back into coding standards, checklists, tests, and safer interfaces.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.320
The mature response to a buffer defect is not only a local patch.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.220
It is improving the habits that allowed the defect to survive review.
